Assuming  $f(x)$  is continuous and differentiable over the reals, let us differentiate the functional equation with respect to  $x$  and  $y$  to obtain: $f(x) - f(y) + (x+y)f'(x) = f(x+y) + (x-y)f'(x+y)$  (i), $f(x) - f(y) - (x+y)f'(y) = -f(x+y) + (x-y)f'(x+y)$  (ii)

and subtracting (ii) from (i) gives:  $(x+y)[f'(x) + f'(y)] = 2f(x+y)$  (iii).  Next, we differentiate (iii) with to respect to  $x$  and  $y$  to get: $f'(x) + f'(y) + (x+y)f''(x) = 2f'(x+y)$  (iv), $f'(x) + f'(y) + (x+y)f''(y) = 2f'(x+y)$  (v) 

and equating (iv) with (v) ultimately yields  $f''(x) = f''(y) =$  constant, or  $f(x) = Ax^2 + Bx + C$  (vi) for real constants  $A, B, C$ .  Substituting (iv) back into the original function equation now produces: $(x+y)[Ax^2 + Bx + C - Ay^2 - By - C] = (x-y)[A(x+y)^2 + B(x+y) + C]$ ;

or  $Ax^3 + Bx^2 -Axy^2 - Bxy + Ax^2y + Bxy - Ay^3 - By^2 = Ax^3 + 2Ax^2y + Axy^2 + Bx^2 + Bxy + Cx - Ax^2y - 2Axy^2 - Ay^3 - Bxy - By^2 - Cy$ ;

which entirely simplifies into  $0 = C(x - y)  \Rightarrow C = 0$  for all real  $x$  and  $y$ .  Therefore, the solution is the set  $f(x) = Ax^2 + Bx$  for all  $x \in \mathbb{R}$ .

